    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d558">

                <pb facs="0844" n="826" sameAs="frus:frus1946v05/pg826"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 EW/1–2546: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Kennan</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-01-25T18:00:00+03:00">January 25, 1946—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 25—5:23 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>236. ReEmb’s 131, Jan. 14.<anchor n="7" xml:id="d558-f1"/> Subject revision of
                    Italian armistice regime. Dekanosov has now replied to our second approach in
                    letter dated Jan 24 as follows: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“In reply to your letter of Jan 14, I have been directed to inform you
                            that the point of view of the Soviet Govt outlined in the letter of Jan
                            3 by no means signifies that the Soviet Govt is negatively disposed in
                            principle to a revision of the armistice regime for Italy. On the
                            contrary it is positively disposed toward measures which in this or that
                            degree might relieve the position of Italy. The Soviet Govt as has
                            already been stated in the above mentioned letter believes that the
                            question of a revision of the armistice terms for Italy at this time has
                                <pb facs="0845" n="827" xml:id="pg827"/> lost pertinence in
                            connection with the forthcoming conclusion of a peace treaty with Italy
                            in the near future.</p>
                        <p>Nevertheless taking into account the wishes of the American Govt the
                            Soviet Govt is ready to consider those changes in the armistice
                            conditions for Italy which the American Govt has in mind
                        proposing.”</p></quote></p>
                <p>Sent Dept 236, repeated Home 4, Caserta 3, Paris 24, London 44.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kennan</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d558-f1" n="7" xml:id="d558-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; in this telegram Kennan reported that he had that day addressed a
                    second letter to Dekanozov (740.00119 EW/1–1446).</p>
            </note>
